2	Application Flow Manual	Application Flow Manual\nOverview\nThis document describes the end-to-end application flow for the L1 Operation Analyst project. It details how data moves from upstream systems through the ingestion and processing pipeline into downstream systems, how agents interact with the knowledge base, and the operational controls required to keep the pipeline healthy. The manual is written for engineers, L1 analysts, and system operators who will run, monitor, and troubleshoot the system.\nComponents\nCore AWS Simulation Components\nS3 ÔÇö landing and processed buckets for raw and transformed data.\nSNS ÔÇö event notification for object creation and pipeline events.\nLambda ÔÇö lightweight validation, orchestration, and trigger logic.\nGlue ÔÇö ETL jobs for schema transformation and catalog registration.\nEMR ÔÇö Spark processing for heavy transformations and ML workloads.\nAthena ÔÇö ad hoc SQL queries over S3 processed data.\nSnowflake ÔÇö downstream data warehouse for analytics and reporting.\nPostgres with pgvector ÔÇö knowledge base storing incidents, RCAs, and embeddings.\nAgents ÔÇö Analyzer, Validator, Escalator orchestrated by LangChain or equivalent.\nUpstream Systems\nMainframe ÔÇö batch exports delivered as fixed width or CSV files.\nInformatica ÔÇö scheduled ETL jobs that push curated datasets.\nSalesforce ÔÇö CRM exports in CSV or JSON format.\nDownstream Systems\nSnowflake ÔÇö final analytics store.\nAWS Data Lake ÔÇö S3 processed layer and Glue catalog.\nSalesforce ÔÇö downstream updates for enriched records.\nEnd to End Flow\nThis section describes the canonical sequence of events from file arrival to downstream availability and KB update.\nSequence Overview\nFile Arrival\nUpstream system writes file to S3 landing bucket.\nS3 generates an ObjectCreated event.\nEvent Notification\nS3 event publishes to SNS topic configured for the bucket and prefix filters.\nLambda Validation\nSNS triggers Lambda.\nLambda performs lightweight validation: file extension, schema header check, file size threshold.\nIf validation fails, Lambda writes a failure event to a monitoring topic and optionally moves file to quarantine bucket.\nOrchestration\nOn successful validation, Lambda triggers Glue job or EMR step with job parameters (S3 path, schema, partition keys).\nProcessing\nGlue or EMR executes ETL/Spark job: parse, transform, enrich, write processed data to S3 processed bucket and register partitions in Glue catalog.\nJobs emit logs and metrics to CloudWatch or local log store.\nCatalog Registration\nGlue job updates Glue Data Catalog with table schema and partitions.\nIf partition discovery is required, run MSCK REPAIR TABLE or explicit partition registration.\nDownstream Load\nProcessed data is loaded into Snowflake via Snowpipe or bulk copy, or made available to Athena for ad hoc queries.\nMonitoring and KB Update\nIf job fails, logs are captured and an incident record is created in Postgres KB.\nAnalyzer Agent generates an RCA draft using KB semantic search and stores the draft in the KB.\nValidator Agent reviews and marks validated or escalates.\nEscalator Agent notifies L2 or downstream owners via email, Slack, or ticketing system.\nTextual Sequence Diagram\nCode\nMainframe/Informatica/Salesforce -> S3 Landing\nS3 Landing -> SNS Topic\nSNS Topic -> Lambda Validator\nLambda Validator -> Glue/EMR Trigger\nGlue/EMR -> S3 Processed\nGlue -> Glue Catalog\nS3 Processed -> Athena / Snowflake\nGlue/EMR -> Logs -> KB Incident Creation\nKB -> Analyzer Agent -> RCA Draft\nRCA Draft -> Validator Agent -> Validated or Escalate\nEscalate -> L2 / Ticketing / Slack\nData Contracts and Event Schemas\nS3 Event Notification Filter Example\njson\n{\n  "Records": [\n    {\n      "eventVersion": "2.1",\n      "eventSource": "aws:s3",\n      "s3": {\n        "bucket": { "name": "landing-bucket" },\n        "object": { "key": "inbound/mainframe/2026-08-23/file_001.csv" }\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\nLambda Validation Payload\njson\n{\n  "bucket": "landing-bucket",\n  "key": "inbound/mainframe/2026-08-23/file_001.csv",\n  "size": 12345678,\n  "metadata": {\n    "source_system": "mainframe",\n    "expected_schema": "mainframe_schema_v2"\n  }\n}\nGlue Job Parameters Example\njson\n{\n  "JobName": "glue_transform_mainframe",\n  "Arguments": {\n    "--S3_INPUT": "s3://landing-bucket/inbound/mainframe/2026-08-23/file_001.csv",\n    "--S3_OUTPUT": "s3://processed-bucket/mainframe/2026-08-23/",\n    "--PARTITION_KEYS": "year,month,day",\n    "--SCHEMA_VERSION": "v2"\n  }\n}\nAthena Table DDL Example\nsql\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE IF NOT EXISTS processed.mainframe_data (\n  id string,\n  account_number string,\n  transaction_date string,\n  amount double\n)\nPARTITIONED BY (year string, month string, day string)\nROW FORMAT SERDE 'org.apache.hadoop.hive.serde2.lazy.LazySimpleSerDe'\nWITH SERDEPROPERTIES ('serialization.format' = ',')\nLOCATION 's3://processed-bucket/mainframe/';\nAgent Workflows\nAnalyzer Agent\nInput: Job logs, error messages, raw stack traces.\nActions:\nGenerate embedding for incoming error text.\nQuery KB using semantic similarity to retrieve top N related incidents.\nAggregate candidate RCAs and produce a draft RCA with probable root cause, suggested remediation, and confidence score.\nInsert draft into KB with validated = FALSE.\nValidator Agent\nInput: Draft RCA and supporting evidence.\nActions:\nCross-check draft against logs and system metrics.\nValidate suggested remediation steps are safe and feasible.\nMark RCA as validated = TRUE or add comments and reassign to Analyzer for revision.\nEscalator Agent\nInput: Validated RCA.\nActions:\nCreate ticket in ticketing system or send notification to L2 owners.\nAttach logs, KB references, and remediation steps.\nUpdate incident status in KB and track SLA.\nError Handling and Retry Patterns\nLambda Validation Failures\nBehavior: Move file to quarantine bucket, publish failure event to monitoring SNS, create KB incident.\nRetry: No automatic retry for malformed files. Notify upstream owner.\nGlue/EMR Job Failures\nBehavior: Capture job logs, create KB incident, attempt automatic retry up to N times for transient errors.\nRetry Policy:\nTransient network or resource errors: retry 2 times with exponential backoff.\nSchema or code errors: do not retry automatically; require manual intervention.\nCatalog Sync Failures\nBehavior: If Glue catalog update fails, mark processed data as unregistered and create KB incident.\nRemediation: Run MSCK REPAIR TABLE or explicit partition registration.\nDownstream Load Failures\nBehavior: If Snowflake load fails, keep processed files in staging area and create KB incident.\nRemediation: Re-run Snowpipe or bulk load after fixing schema or permission issues.\nMonitoring and Observability\nLogs\nGlue/EMR: driver and executor logs.\nLambda: function logs and invocation metrics.\nAgents: agent execution logs and KB write operations.\nMetrics to Monitor\nFile arrival rate and missing file alerts.\nJob success rate for Glue and EMR.\nAverage job duration and tail latency.\nAthena query latency and failure rate.\nKB ingestion success for embeddings.\nAgent RCA validation rate and time to validation.\nAlerting Rules\nMissing file SLA: alert if expected file not received within SLA window.\nJob failure threshold: alert if more than X jobs fail in Y minutes.\nHigh query latency: alert when Athena queries exceed threshold.\nKB ingestion failure: alert on repeated embedding generation failures.\nSuggested Alert Channels\nCritical: PagerDuty or oncall Slack channel.\nHigh: Team Slack channel and email.\nMedium: Email and ticket creation.\nDeployment and CI CD\nSource Control\nKeep Glue scripts, EMR bootstrap scripts, Lambda code, and SQL DDL in Git.\nTag releases and maintain deployment branches.\nCI Steps\nLint and unit tests for Python code and Spark jobs.\nBuild Lambda deployment package and Docker images if used.\nIntegration tests using a staging S3 bucket and test Glue jobs.\nDeploy via IaC (CloudFormation, Terraform) or scripted CLI.\nRollback Strategy\nMaintain previous stable Glue job script and Lambda package.\nIf new deployment causes failures, revert to previous version and open a rollback incident.\nSecurity and Access Control\nIAM Best Practices\nUse least privilege for Lambda, Glue, and EMR roles.\nSeparate roles for read only and write operations to S3 and Glue.\nUse resource policies for cross account access.\nData Protection\nEncrypt S3 buckets with SSE KMS.\nUse TLS for all service endpoints.\nRotate credentials and use secrets manager for DB passwords.\nKB Access\nCreate DB roles for agents and human operators with least privilege.\nUse SSL for Postgres connections where possible.\nTesting and Validation\nUnit Tests\nTest parsing logic for each upstream file format.\nTest Lambda validation logic with positive and negative cases.\nIntegration Tests\nSimulate file arrival in staging S3 and validate end to end flow through Glue/EMR to Athena and Snowflake.\nValidate agent behavior by injecting synthetic errors and verifying RCA generation and validation.\nPerformance Tests\nLoad test Glue/EMR jobs with large files and measure scaling behavior.\nTest embedding generation throughput and Postgres vector search latency.\nTroubleshooting Playbook\nIf file never triggers pipeline\nCheck S3 event configuration and SNS subscription.\nVerify object key prefix and suffix filters.\nConfirm SNS topic has active subscription to Lambda.\nIf Lambda fails to invoke Glue\nVerify Lambda IAM role has glue:StartJobRun permission.\nConfirm Glue job name and arguments passed by Lambda.\nIf Glue job fails with library errors\nCheck job Python library path and wheel locations.\nFor EMR, verify bootstrap actions installed required jars and Python packages.\nIf Athena queries fail\nVerify Glue catalog schema matches S3 data.\nRun MSCK REPAIR TABLE or ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION.\nIf KB semantic search returns poor matches\nConfirm embedding model dimension matches incident_embeddings.embedding vector size.\nRe-seed embeddings after changing model.\nIncrease number of retrieved neighbors and apply additional filtering.\nOperational Checklists\nDaily Checklist\nVerify scheduled jobs completed successfully.\nCheck KB ingestion pipeline health.\nReview critical alerts and unresolved incidents.\nWeekly Checklist\nRun catalog consistency checks.\nValidate Snowflake loads and downstream reports.\nReview agent validation metrics and false positive rates.\nMonthly Checklist\nRotate credentials and review IAM policies.\nArchive old incidents and backup Postgres DB.\nReview and update runbooks based on recent incidents.\nAppendix A Sample Commands\nbash\n# Run schema\npsql -h localhost -U postgres -d kb_db -f sql/001_init_schema.sql\n# Seed incidents\npsql -h localhost -U postgres -d kb_db -f sql/002_seed_incidents.sql\n# Run Glue job via AWS CLI\naws glue start-job-run --job-name glue_transform_mainframe --arguments '--S3_INPUT=s3://landing-bucket/...'\n# Repair Athena partitions\naws athena start-query-execution --query-string "MSCK REPAIR TABLE processed.mainframe_data;" --result-configuration OutputLocation=s3://athena-results/\n# Check Docker container logs\ndocker logs pgvector-local\nAppendix B Sample SQL Queries\nsql\n-- Find recent failed incidents\nSELECT * FROM incidents WHERE validated = FALSE ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50;\n-- Semantic similarity query example\nSELECT i.*, e.embedding <-> '[0.12, -0.23, ...]'::vector AS distance\nFROM incidents i\nJOIN incident_embeddings e ON i.incident_id = e.incident_id\nORDER BY distance\nLIMIT 5;\nFinal Notes\nKeep the flow diagrams and runbooks updated after each major incident.\nAutomate repetitive checks to reduce manual triage time.\nVersion control all pipeline code and configuration.\nUse the KB to continuously improve agent accuracy and reduce mean time to resolution.	{}	flow	D:\\vsworkspace\\L1_Operation_Analyst\\backend\\docs\\Application_Flow_Manual.docx	\N	2026-09-05 08:07:02.218866
3	Project Architecture Flow Manual	Project Architecture Flow Manual\nOverview\nThis document describes the project architecture for the L1 Operation Analyst system. It explains components, integration points, data flows, and operational controls. The manual is written so architects, SREs, L1/L2 engineers, and technical writers can reproduce diagrams, embed them into a formal document, and use the content as the authoritative architecture reference.\nArchitecture Diagrams\nBelow are the required diagrams, each with a clear purpose, a detailed visual specification, and step-by-step guidance for creating the diagram in a word processor or diagram tool. Insert each diagram at the indicated location in the document and include the provided caption and alt text.\nDiagram 1 Overview Architecture\nPurpose High-level system map showing upstream systems, ingestion pipeline, processing layer, knowledge base, agents, and downstream systems.\nVisual elements\nUpstream systems (three icons): Mainframe, Informatica, Salesforce.\nIngestion layer: S3 landing bucket icon.\nEvent bus: SNS icon.\nOrchestration: Lambda icon.\nProcessing: Glue and EMR icons grouped.\nStorage and catalog: S3 processed bucket and Glue Data Catalog icons.\nQuery layer: Athena icon.\nKnowledge base: Postgres with vector store icon.\nAgents: Analyzer, Validator, Escalator icons with arrows to/from KB.\nDownstream systems: Snowflake and Salesforce icons.\nNetwork and security: IAM and VPC boundary drawn as a dashed box.\nLayout guidance\nPlace upstream systems on the left, downstream on the right, processing and KB in the center.\nUse directional arrows to show flow: upstream ÔåÆ S3 ÔåÆ SNS ÔåÆ Lambda ÔåÆ Glue/EMR ÔåÆ S3 processed ÔåÆ Athena/Snowflake.\nUse color coding: blue for data movement, green for compute, orange for storage, purple for agents.\nCaption Overview architecture showing upstream sources, ingestion pipeline, processing, knowledge base, agents, and downstream targets.\nAlt text High-level architecture diagram mapping upstream systems to ingestion, processing, knowledge base, agents, and downstream systems.\nDiagram 2 Detailed Ingestion Flow\nPurpose Zoom into the ingestion path: file arrival, validation, quarantine, and trigger logic.\nVisual elements\nFile producer icon (Mainframe/Informatica/Salesforce).\nS3 landing bucket with object key example.\nSNS topic with subscription to Lambda.\nLambda validation function with decision diamond: pass / fail.\nQuarantine bucket for failed validation.\nTrigger to Glue/EMR for passed validation.\nLayout guidance\nUse a swimlane style: Producer lane, Ingestion lane, Validation lane, Processing lane.\nAnnotate decision points with conditions (file extension, schema header, size threshold).\nAdd small text boxes with example event payload fields: bucket, key, size, metadata.\nCaption Detailed ingestion flow showing validation, quarantine, and trigger to processing.\nAlt text Ingestion flow diagram with validation and quarantine steps before processing.\nDiagram 3 Processing and Catalog Registration\nPurpose Show Glue/EMR processing steps, partition registration, and catalog updates.\nVisual elements\nGlue job and EMR cluster boxes.\nSteps inside processing: parse ÔåÆ transform ÔåÆ enrich ÔåÆ write.\nArrow to S3 processed bucket.\nArrow from processing to Glue Data Catalog with partition registration step.\nOptional Snowflake load path (Snowpipe or bulk copy).\nLayout guidance\nRepresent processing as a multi-step pipeline inside a single box.\nShow partition registration as a separate action with a callout explaining MSCK REPAIR TABLE or explicit partition add.\nCaption Processing pipeline and catalog registration flow for processed data.\nAlt text Processing pipeline diagram showing ETL steps and catalog registration.\nDiagram 4 Knowledge Base and Agent Interaction\nPurpose Illustrate how agents interact with the KB, generate embeddings, and perform semantic search.\nVisual elements\nPostgres KB box with two subcomponents: incidents table and embeddings vector store.\nEmbedding generator (model) icon.\nAnalyzer Agent flow: input logs ÔåÆ embedding ÔåÆ similarity search ÔåÆ draft RCA.\nValidator Agent flow: review draft ÔåÆ mark validated.\nEscalator Agent flow: create ticket / notify L2.\nLayout guidance\nUse arrows to show data movement: logs ÔåÆ embedding model ÔåÆ KB; KB ÔåÆ Analyzer ÔåÆ draft ÔåÆ Validator ÔåÆ Escalator.\nAnnotate the embedding dimension and example similarity query.\nCaption Agent workflows interacting with the knowledge base for RCA generation and escalation.\nAlt text Diagram showing agents, embedding generation, and KB interactions.\nDiagram 5 Security and Network Boundaries\nPurpose Depict IAM roles, VPC boundaries, encryption, and access control for sensitive components.\nVisual elements\nVPC boundary box containing Glue/EMR, Lambda, and Postgres (if self-hosted).\nIAM role icons attached to Lambda, Glue, and EMR.\nS3 bucket policies and KMS key icon for encryption.\nArrows showing secure connections (TLS) and restricted access to Snowflake.\nLayout guidance\nUse a legend for icons: IAM, KMS, VPC, TLS.\nAnnotate least privilege rules and recommended role permissions.\nCaption Security architecture showing network boundaries, IAM roles, and encryption.\nAlt text Security diagram with VPC, IAM roles, and encryption components.\nComponent Specifications\nThis section lists each component, responsibilities, configuration notes, and operational parameters.\nStorage and Catalog\nS3 Landing\nPurpose: raw file ingestion.\nRecommended lifecycle: keep raw for 90 days, then archive.\nEvent configuration: ObjectCreated with prefix and suffix filters.\nS3 Processed\nPurpose: processed/parquet output.\nPartitioning: year/month/day or business keys.\nEncryption: SSE-KMS recommended.\nGlue Data Catalog\nPurpose: table metadata for Athena and downstream consumers.\nBest practice: version schema changes and run partition repair after ETL.\nCompute\nLambda\nPurpose: validation, orchestration, lightweight transforms.\nTimeout: set based on validation complexity; default 30s, increase if needed.\nIAM: minimal role with S3 read, SNS subscribe, Glue start job permissions.\nGlue\nPurpose: serverless ETL for schema transformations.\nWorker type: G.1X or G.2X depending on workload.\nLibraries: include external Python wheels via S3 path.\nEMR\nPurpose: heavy Spark workloads and ML processing.\nInstance sizing: tune executor memory and cores; use spot instances for cost optimization.\nKnowledge Base\nPostgres with Vector Extension\nSchema: incidents, incident_embeddings, architecture_docs.\nEmbedding column: vector(N) where N equals model dimension.\nBackup: regular dumps and offsite storage.\nAgents\nAnalyzer\nInputs: logs, error messages.\nOutputs: draft RCA with confidence score.\nValidator\nInputs: draft RCA and evidence.\nOutputs: validated RCA or feedback.\nEscalator\nInputs: validated RCA.\nOutputs: ticket creation, notifications.\nData Flow and Sequence Diagrams\nProvide textual sequence diagrams and sample payloads to include alongside visuals.\nSequence Diagram Text\nCode\nUpstream -> S3 Landing: upload file\nS3 Landing -> SNS: publish event\nSNS -> Lambda: invoke validation\nLambda -> Quarantine: move if invalid\nLambda -> Glue/EMR: start job if valid\nGlue/EMR -> S3 Processed: write output\nGlue -> Glue Catalog: update schema and partitions\nS3 Processed -> Athena: queryable data\nGlue/EMR -> Logs -> KB: create incident on failure\nKB -> Analyzer: semantic search and draft RCA\nAnalyzer -> Validator: submit draft\nValidator -> Escalator: validated RCA triggers ticket\nEscalator -> L2: notify and attach evidence\nExample Event Payload\nS3 event: bucket, key, size, etag, metadata.source_system.\nLambda validation result: status, errors[], recommended action.\nGlue job arguments: S3 input path, S3 output path, partition keys, schema version.\nDeployment and Operations\nThis section covers deployment patterns, CI/CD, monitoring, and runbook integration.\nCI/CD Recommendations\nStore Glue scripts, Lambda code, and SQL DDL in source control.\nUse automated pipelines to build and test artifacts.\nDeploy via IaC templates or CLI scripts to ensure repeatability.\nMonitoring and Observability\nLogs: centralize in CloudWatch or equivalent.\nMetrics: job success rate, job duration, KB ingestion latency, embedding generation throughput.\nAlerts: missing file SLA, repeated job failures, KB ingestion errors.\nBackup and Recovery\nPostgres: scheduled dumps and retention policy.\nS3: lifecycle rules and cross-region replication for DR.\nGlue/EMR: keep job definitions in version control for quick redeploy.\nAppendix\nProvide assets descriptions, diagram creation tips, and a checklist for finalizing the document.\nDiagram Asset Descriptions\nFor each diagram include:\nTitle: short descriptive title.\nPurpose: one sentence.\nElements: list of icons and labels to include.\nAnnotations: text to place near arrows or decision points.\nSuggested caption: one line for the figure caption.\nAlt text: short accessibility description.\nDiagram Creation Tips\nUse a consistent icon set and color palette across all diagrams.\nKeep text labels concise; use callouts for longer explanations.\nExport diagrams at high resolution for PDF and Word embedding.\nFor complex flows, create a zoomed overview and separate detailed sub-diagrams.\nDocument Checklist Before Release\n[ ] All diagrams inserted with captions and alt text.\n[ ] Architecture decisions documented and versioned.\n[ ] IAM roles and least privilege rules listed.\n[ ] Backup and DR procedures validated.\n[ ] Runbooks linked to each diagram and component.\n[ ] Document reviewed by system owner and security team.\nHow to use this content in a Word document\nCreate a new Word document and use the headings above as section headings.\nFor each diagram placeholder, insert a high-resolution image or recreate the diagram using Word shapes or a diagram tool.\nAdd figure numbers and captions under each diagram.\nInclude a table of contents generated from the document headings.\nSave the final document as the project architecture manual and export to PDF for distribution.\nEnd of Project Architecture Flow Manual	{}	architecture	D:\\vsworkspace\\L1_Operation_Analyst\\backend\\docs\\Project_Architecture_Flow_Manual.docx	\N	2026-09-05 08:07:02.255774
