5	Application Support Manual	Application Support Manual\nOverview\nThis document is the Application Support Manual for the L1 Operation Analyst project. It provides comprehensive, operational guidance for support engineers to monitor, diagnose, remediate, and escalate incidents across the pipeline that integrates upstream systems (Mainframe, Informatica, Salesforce) with downstream systems (Snowflake, AWS Data Lake / S3, Athena, Salesforce). It covers environment setup, runbooks for common failure types, database and knowledge-base management, monitoring and alerting, security, maintenance, and appendices with ready-to-use commands and SQL.\nSupported Systems\nUpstream systems\nMainframe ÔÇö batch file exports (fixed width, CSV) delivered to landing S3 buckets.\nInformatica ÔÇö ETL jobs that push curated datasets to S3 or Snowflake.\nSalesforce (upstream) ÔÇö CRM exports (CSV/JSON) that feed enrichment pipelines.\nDownstream systems\nSnowflake ÔÇö cloud data warehouse for analytics and reporting.\nAWS Data Lake ÔÇö S3 buckets for raw/processed data, Glue catalog for metadata, Athena for ad-hoc queries.\nSalesforce (downstream) ÔÇö enriched records pushed back to Salesforce for business users.\nCore platform components\nS3 ÔÇö file landing and storage.\nSNS ÔÇö event notifications for file arrivals.\nLambda ÔÇö lightweight orchestration and triggers.\nGlue / EMR ÔÇö ETL and Spark processing.\nAthena ÔÇö SQL query layer over S3.\nPostgres + pgvector ÔÇö knowledge base (KB) storing incidents, metadata, and embeddings.\npgAdmin4 / psql ÔÇö DB administration tools.\nAgents ÔÇö Analyzer, Validator, Escalator (orchestrated via LangChain or equivalent).\nArchitecture and Data Flow (summary)\nHigh level flow\nFile arrival: Upstream system drops file into S3 landing bucket.\nEvent notification: S3 event ÔåÆ SNS topic.\nTrigger: SNS ÔåÆ Lambda (validation, lightweight parsing).\nProcessing: Lambda triggers Glue/EMR job for heavy ETL.\nOutput: Processed data written to S3 (processed) and/or Snowflake.\nQuery: Athena reads processed S3 data for analysts.\nKB: Incident logs and RCA stored in Postgres; embeddings stored in pgvector column for semantic search.\nAgents: Analyzer queries KB, drafts RCA; Validator verifies; Escalator notifies L2 or downstream owners.\nKey integration points\nS3 Ôåö SNS: event filters must match expected file extensions and prefixes.\nLambda Ôåö Glue/EMR: correct ARNs and IAM permissions required.\nGlue Catalog Ôåö Athena: schema must be registered and partitioned correctly.\nPostgres Ôåö Agents: embedding dimension must match model output (see Appendix).\nInstallation and Setup\nRepository layout (recommended)\nCode\n/l1_operation_analyst/\n  Ôö£ÔöÇ docker-compose.yml\n  Ôö£ÔöÇ sql/\n  Ôöé   Ôö£ÔöÇ 001_init_schema.sql\n  Ôöé   Ôö£ÔöÇ 002_seed_incidents.sql\n  Ôö£ÔöÇ scripts/\n  Ôöé   Ôö£ÔöÇ seed_embeddings.py\n  Ôöé   Ôö£ÔöÇ query_kb.py\n  Ôö£ÔöÇ docs/\n  ÔööÔöÇ docs/images/\nPostgres (pgvector) via Docker\ndocker-compose.yml example:\nyaml\nversion: "3.8"\nservices:\n  postgres:\n    image: ankane/pgvector:latest\n    container_name: pgvector-local\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: postgres\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: postgres\n      POSTGRES_DB: kb_db\n    ports:\n      - "5432:5432"\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\nImportant: If you change POSTGRES_PASSWORD, remove the volume and recreate the container:\nbash\ndocker compose down -v\ndocker compose up -d\nDatabase initialization\nRun schema:\nbash\npsql -h localhost -U postgres -d kb_db -f sql/001_init_schema.sql\nSeed incidents:\nbash\npsql -h localhost -U postgres -d kb_db -f sql/002_seed_incidents.sql\npgAdmin connection\nHost: localhost\nPort: 5432\nUser: postgres\nDatabase: kb_db\nEmbedding model\nChoose model and note dimension (vector length). Examples:\ntext-embedding-ada-002 ÔåÆ 1536\nall-MiniLM-L6-v2 ÔåÆ 384\nEnsure incident_embeddings.embedding column uses vector(N) where N equals model dimension.\nOperational Runbooks (by incident type)\nEach runbook below follows the same structure: Symptoms ÔåÆ Immediate checks ÔåÆ Root cause checks ÔåÆ Remediation ÔåÆ Post-mortem actions.\n1. Python libraries not available\nSymptoms\nModuleNotFoundError or ImportError in Glue/EMR/Lambda logs.\nImmediate checks\nConfirm job/container logs (CloudWatch or local logs).\nCheck deployment package / Glue job Python library list.\nRoot cause checks\nWas the library included in the job package or Glue Python library path?\nFor EMR, check bootstrap actions and cluster libraries.\nFor Lambda, check layers and deployment zip.\nRemediation\nAdd missing library to requirements.txt and rebuild package.\nFor Lambda, create/update a Lambda Layer and attach.\nFor Glue, add library to job parameters or use an S3 wheel path.\nPost-mortem\nUpdate deployment checklist and CI packaging to include dependency verification.\n2. Python libraries version mismatch\nSymptoms\nAttributeError, TypeError, or runtime incompatibilities.\nImmediate checks\nInspect pip freeze or package versions in runtime environment.\nCheck requirements.txt and lockfile.\nRoot cause checks\nConflicting transitive dependencies.\nDifferent environments (dev vs prod) using different versions.\nRemediation\nPin versions in requirements.txt or use a lockfile.\nRebuild and redeploy with consistent versions.\nPost-mortem\nAdd automated dependency checks to CI.\n3. Code compilation issues\nSymptoms\nSyntaxError, NameError, or compile-time exceptions.\nImmediate checks\nRun linter/static analysis locally.\nCheck the failing line in logs.\nRemediation\nFix code, run unit tests, redeploy.\nPost-mortem\nAdd pre-deploy linting and unit tests.\n4. File landed with wrong extension\nSymptoms\nIngestion job fails to parse file; event filter mismatch.\nImmediate checks\nInspect S3 object key and extension.\nCheck S3 event notification filter configuration.\nRemediation\nMove file to correct bucket or update ingestion to accept extension.\nNotify upstream owner to correct export process.\nPost-mortem\nAdd validation Lambda to reject unsupported extensions and notify upstream.\n5. SNS and Lambda integration failure\nSymptoms\nSNS shows delivery failures; Lambda not invoked.\nImmediate checks\nCheck SNS subscription status and CloudWatch logs.\nVerify Lambda function is enabled.\nRoot cause checks\nIAM permissions for SNS to invoke Lambda.\nEndpoint configuration and subscription confirmation.\nRemediation\nUpdate IAM policy to allow sns:Publish/lambda:InvokeFunction.\nRe-subscribe or manually confirm subscription.\nPost-mortem\nAdd subscription health checks and alerting.\n6. Lambda and EMR/Glue trigger failure\nSymptoms\nLambda logs show failure to start Glue/EMR job.\nImmediate checks\nValidate Glue job ARN or EMR cluster ID.\nCheck IAM role used by Lambda.\nRemediation\nCorrect ARNs, update IAM role permissions, ensure cluster exists.\nPost-mortem\nAdd integration tests for trigger flows.\n7. File size too high for EMR/Glue\nSymptoms\nOutOfMemoryError, job timeouts, or long runtimes.\nImmediate checks\nCheck file size in S3 and job memory metrics.\nRemediation\nPartition or chunk large files.\nIncrease cluster resources or worker memory.\nUse streaming processing where possible.\nPost-mortem\nAdd file size thresholds and pre-processing to split large files.\n8. Athena table not queryable\nSymptoms\nHIVE_PARTITION_SCHEMA_MISMATCH, column not found, or query timeouts.\nImmediate checks\nVerify Glue catalog schema and partitions.\nCheck S3 data format and partition layout.\nRemediation\nRun MSCK REPAIR TABLE or ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION.\nUpdate Glue catalog schema to match data.\nPost-mortem\nAdd catalog sync job after ETL completes.\n9. Athena table not accessible to end user\nSymptoms\nAccessDeniedException for SELECT queries.\nImmediate checks\nCheck IAM policies and resource-based policies for Athena and Glue.\nRemediation\nGrant athena:StartQueryExecution and S3 read permissions to user role.\nUpdate Glue catalog permissions.\nPost-mortem\nDocument access provisioning steps and automate role assignment.\n10. Glue catalog issues\nSymptoms\nMissing partitions, stale metadata, or corrupted catalog entries.\nImmediate checks\nInspect Glue console for table metadata.\nCheck last catalog update timestamp.\nRemediation\nRebuild table metadata, run repair commands, or recreate table if corrupted.\nPost-mortem\nAdd catalog refresh step to ETL pipeline.\n11. Spark job failure\nSymptoms\nOutOfMemoryError, Task not serializable, Shuffle error.\nImmediate checks\nReview Spark executor logs and driver logs.\nCheck data skew and partitioning.\nRemediation\nIncrease executor memory, repartition data, fix serialization issues, add missing jars.\nPost-mortem\nAdd Spark job tuning checklist and monitoring.\nDatabase and Knowledge Base Management\nSchema (example)\nsql\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS incidents (\n  incident_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  service VARCHAR(50),\n  error_log TEXT,\n  root_cause TEXT,\n  resolution TEXT,\n  validated BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,\n  created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS incident_embeddings (\n  incident_id INT REFERENCES incidents(incident_id),\n  embedding vector(1536)\n);\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS architecture_docs (\n  doc_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  title VARCHAR(100),\n  content TEXT,\n  image_path TEXT,\n  created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);\nEmbedding dimension\nEnsure vector(N) matches model output dimension. If using OpenAI text-embedding-ada-002, set N = 1536. If using MiniLM, set N = 384.\nSeeding embeddings (high level)\nRead incidents rows.\nGenerate embeddings with chosen model.\nInsert into incident_embeddings:\nsql\nINSERT INTO incident_embeddings (incident_id, embedding)\nVALUES (123, '[0.123, -0.234, ...]'::vector);\nSample similarity query\nsql\nSELECT i.*, e.embedding <-> '[0.12, -0.23, ...]'::vector AS distance\nFROM incidents i\nJOIN incident_embeddings e ON i.incident_id = e.incident_id\nORDER BY distance\nLIMIT 5;\nImage handling for architecture docs\nStore images in /docs/images/ or S3 and reference via image_path (preferred). Avoid storing binary in DB unless necessary.\nMonitoring, Logging, and Alerts\nLogging\nCentralize logs in CloudWatch (or local file system for simulation).\nEnsure Glue/EMR, Lambda, and application logs are retained for at least 30 days.\nMetrics and alerts\nFile arrival failures: alert when expected file not received within SLA window.\nJob failures: alert on Glue/EMR job failures or repeated retries.\nHigh latency: alert when Athena queries exceed threshold.\nKB ingestion failures: alert when embedding generation fails.\nSuggested alert channels\nPagerDuty or Slack for critical incidents.\nEmail for informational alerts.\nSNS for automated notifications.\nRunbook for alerts\nTriage severity (P0ÔÇôP3).\nAssign L1 owner.\nFollow the relevant runbook above.\nIf unresolved within SLA, escalate to L2.\nTroubleshooting and Diagnostics\nCommon diagnostic commands\nCheck Docker containers:\nbash\ndocker ps\ndocker logs pgvector-local\nConnect to Postgres inside container:\nbash\ndocker exec -it pgvector-local psql -U postgres -d kb_db\nConnect from host:\nbash\npsql -h localhost -U postgres -d kb_db -W\nCheck Glue catalog partitions (example CLI):\nbash\naws glue get-table --database-name mydb --name mytable\nRepair Athena partitions:\nsql\nMSCK REPAIR TABLE my_table;\nWhen connection refused\nVerify port mapping and that no local Postgres is conflicting. Consider remapping to 5433:5432.\nWhen authentication fails\nRecreate volume and container to apply new POSTGRES_PASSWORD:\nbash\ndocker compose down -v\ndocker compose up -d\nSecurity, Access Control, and Compliance\nDatabase access\nUse least privilege for DB users. Create separate read/write roles for agents and admins.\nRotate DB credentials periodically.\nIAM and AWS\nUse fine-grained IAM roles for Lambda, Glue, and EMR.\nEnsure S3 bucket policies restrict access to required principals.\nData protection\nEncrypt S3 at rest (SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS).\nUse SSL/TLS for DB connections where applicable.\nAudit and logging\nEnable CloudTrail for AWS API auditing.\nKeep audit logs for at least 90 days (adjust per compliance).\nMaintenance, Backups, and Disaster Recovery\nDatabase backups\nFor Docker local dev: periodically pg_dump the DB:\nbash\npg_dump -h localhost -U postgres -d kb_db -F c -f backups/kb_db_$(date +%F).dump\nFor production: use managed DB snapshots or scheduled backups.\nRestore\nbash\npg_restore -h localhost -U postgres -d kb_db -v backups/kb_db_2026-08-23.dump\nGlue/EMR\nKeep job definitions in version control.\nUse automated deployment for job scripts and bootstrap actions.\nDisaster recovery\nDocument RTO/RPO.\nKeep offsite backups of DB dumps and critical config files.\nRoles, Responsibilities, and Escalation Matrix\nRoles\nL1 Analyst ÔÇö first responder, triage, runbooks, RCA draft.\nL2 Analyst ÔÇö deep troubleshooting, code fixes, infra changes.\nSystem Admin ÔÇö manage Docker, DB, and environment.\nApplication Owner ÔÇö business owner for escalations.\nEscalation matrix (example)\nContacts\nL1 Team Slack: #l1-ops\nOncall L2: l2-oncall@example.com\nSystem Admin: infra-admin@example.com\nAppendices\nAppendix A ÔÇö Full 001_init_schema.sql\nsql\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS incidents (\n    incident_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    service VARCHAR(50),\n    error_log TEXT,\n    root_cause TEXT,\n    resolution TEXT,\n    validated BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS incident_embeddings (\n    incident_id INT REFERENCES incidents(incident_id),\n    embedding vector(1536)\n);\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS architecture_docs (\n    doc_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    title VARCHAR(100),\n    content TEXT,\n    image_path TEXT,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);\nAppendix B ÔÇö Example seed_embeddings.py (skeleton)\npython\n# seed_embeddings.py (skeleton)\nimport psycopg2\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nMODEL_NAME = "all-MiniLM-L6-v2"  # adjust as needed\nDIM = 384\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(MODEL_NAME)\nconn = psycopg2.connect(host="localhost", dbname="kb_db", user="postgres", password="postgres")\ncur = conn.cursor()\ncur.execute("SELECT incident_id, error_log FROM incidents WHERE incident_id NOT IN (SELECT incident_id FROM incident_embeddings)")\nrows = cur.fetchall()\nfor incident_id, text in rows:\n    emb = model.encode(text).tolist()\n    cur.execute("INSERT INTO incident_embeddings (incident_id, embedding) VALUES (%s, %s)", (incident_id, emb))\nconn.commit()\ncur.close()\nconn.close()\nlist of jobs and their main involved services:\nlocal_develop_run_emr_step:emr, \nlocal_develop_trigger_glue:lambda, \nprocess_file_less_than_300kb:glue,\n daily_aggregate_metrics:lambda, \nhourly_ingest_s3_to_redshift:glue, \nreconcile_user_events:lambda, \narchive_old_logs:s3, \ncleanup_temp_files:lambda, \nnotify_on_failure:sns, \nprocess_image_thumbnails:lambda, \netl_transform_orders:glue, \nstream_events_to_kinesis:kinesis, \nbatch_export_reports:emr,\n validate_schema_on_ingest:lambda, \nreplicate_db_to_s3:glue, \ngenerate_daily_backups:lambda, \nindex_search_documents:lambda,\nprocess_video_transcoding:emr, \nsync_user_profiles:dynamodb, \npurge_stale_sessions:lambda, \nsend_weekly_digest:ses, \nprocess_payment_webhook:lambda, \ningest_thirdparty_api:lambda, \naggregate_clickstream:emr, \nconvert_csv_to_parquet:glue, \nmonitor_queue_depth:sqs, \nretry_failed_jobs:lambda, \ncleanup_orphaned_resources:lambda, \nrefresh_materialized_views:glue, \nnotify_slack_on_deploy:lambda,\nAppendix C ÔÇö Quick checklist for new incidents\n[ ] Confirm file arrival in S3.\n[ ] Check SNS delivery and Lambda invocation.\n[ ] Inspect Glue/EMR job logs.\n[ ] Query Athena for processed data.\n[ ] Search KB for similar incidents (semantic search).\n[ ] Draft RCA and mark validated when confirmed.\n[ ] Escalate if unresolved within SLA.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nQ: How do I change the embedding model later A: Update the model in seed_embeddings.py, note the new dimension, alter incident_embeddings.embedding to vector(new_dim), and re-seed embeddings.\nQ: Where to store architecture images A: Place images in /docs/images/ or S3 and reference via image_path in architecture_docs.\nQ: How to add new runbooks A: Add a new section under Runbooks with Symptoms, Checks, Remediation, and Post-mortem steps. Commit to repo.\nFinal notes\nKeep this manual versioned in the project repository under /docs/Application_Support_Manual.docx (or .md for source).\nUpdate the manual after every major incident post-mortem.\nAutomate repetitive checks (file arrival, subscription health, catalog sync) to reduce manual triage time.\nEnd of Application Support Manual	{}	manual	D:\\vsworkspace\\L1_Operation_Analyst\\backend\\docs\\Application Support Manual.docx	\N	2026-09-06 16:53:03.469942
\.
